WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.700
I really appreciate

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:04.700
that everyone requested

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:05.900
me to do mod,

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:14.160
and hello from the

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.660
rig offer again today,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:42.420
Leaf cutter ants cleaning the refuse out of their nest.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.740
Every single one of these tiny creatures knows where it's going and what it's got to do

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.500
when it gets there.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.300
And furthermore, there are about 10 million more of them in this huge underground nest

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.300
beneath me.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:03.580
They're all members of one highly organized society, but they're not the blindly mechanical

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.500
robotic slaves that we once thought they were.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.700
Indeed, we now know that every insect society is full of conflict, power struggles, and

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:21.820
mutinous.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:28.020
Social insects construct the tallest of all non-human buildings.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:33.140
Like these huge termite hills here in Australia.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.220
They protect their colonies with great ferocity.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:45.140
They increase the size of their societies at an alarming rate.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:52.540
And they're capable of mobilizing huge armies to make wars on their neighbors.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.020
But how did these great communities develop?

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:04.220
Most insects like this little sand wasp here in the deserts of Arizona live solitary lives.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:10.780
This one has just dug a hole in which she is going to lay her eggs.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.920
But then she does something else.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:22.300
She will cater for her as yet unhatched young by putting a caterpillar inside that hole

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.780
on which they can feed.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:32.660
And that is a very important stage in the development of the social life.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:39.840
In fact, it's the very basis on which all the great insect societies are built.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:47.300
This species of wasp, however, is still at the stage of working alone.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:55.060
After stocking each nest with a caterpillar, she blocks the entrance to deter thieves.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.900
A burrow may be several centimeters deep.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:05.440
At the bottom lies the paralyzed caterpillar and on its back there is now a wasp grub feeding

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.440
on it.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.700
The female wasp makes several of these nests a few feet apart and stocks each of them with

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.820
living food for her young.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:23.060
Can there be a more hardworking mother?

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:30.820
Despite all her attempts at parental care, the vast majority of her young will not survive.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:40.380
She's too busy hunting for more caterpillars to be able to guard all her nest sites.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:46.780
Back in the distant evolutionary past, other wasps started to build their nests alongside

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.060
one another.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:53.180
And here on the coast of Panama, paper wasps still do so.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.260
Grouping their cells together means that even though you have to leave your eggs to collect

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:03.180
food, there will always be someone around on guard.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:10.000
The wasps are all sisters, but as often happens, one tends to dominate the rest.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.340
She starts to bite her sisters with great brutality.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:15.340
She is the boss, the queen.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:22.660
The others may build cells, but only she will lay eggs in them.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.740
Many of the genes in these eggs are the same as those carried by her sisters.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.700
And the sisters look after the eggs as if they were their own.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:36.740
And now, because the nest is so well guarded, the family rears more young than if each female

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.020
were to nest alone.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.360
So as each egg is laid, the sisters take steps to protect it.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:50.180
To do that, they need building material.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:55.900
They chew wood into pulp and then use it to build a protective wall around each egg, a

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:56.900
cell.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:01.620
So a colonial nest begins to grow.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:26.820
With more and more young females needing to be fed, the adults go hunting.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:44.740
Each returning wasp bringing prey is greeted by the other workers.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.460
They squabble over food.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.780
The queen takes the lion's share.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:56.100
Those of her sisters and daughters who are high up on the social scale also get big helpings

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:07.300
because they bully the junior females.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:11.200
In fact, the food isn't eaten by the adult who wins it.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:26.680
She feeds it to her developing younger sisters.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:32.400
This grouping, an enormous single-sex family, was the first step towards the development

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:42.060
of insect societies containing millions of individuals, and it's still their basic structure.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:49.120
The forests in which the first wasps hunted were dominated by horsetails and conifers.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.100
They relied upon the wind to distribute their pollen.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:59.340
But then about 100 million years ago, a new kind of plant appeared which recruited insects

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:06.860
to do the job, and they did it with net-a-loaded flowers.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:12.520
Some of these recruits then abandoned hunting and concentrated instead on this new food.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:17.300
They became bees.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:26.860
Today there are about 20,000 different species of them.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:33.680
This queen bumblebee mated at the end of last summer before she hibernated, but now she's

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:43.420
gone off to look for a new home because she's ready at last to lay those eggs.

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:52.460
She may take some time to find just the right place.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:57.140
A deserted mouse hole.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:58.520
Ideal.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:04.180
First she makes a little wax pot in which she lays a group of fertilized eggs.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:07.080
In due time, these hatch into young females.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.620
The queen now has her subjects.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:12.700
A colony has been established.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.820
From now on, she does little building herself.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:20.660
Her daughters take on that job, and they use a material that no wasp ever had.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:23.260
It oozes from between their body segments.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.740
It's wax.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:31.140
The queen also produces a chemical substance that permeates the nest and keeps her daughters

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:33.460
sexuality in check.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:42.420
Their job is not to produce eggs, but to look after their younger sisters.

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:55.540
More and more young workers are hauling themselves out of their cells.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:09:01.220
They don't have to travel far to find their first adult meal.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:06.700
In fact, to begin with, they stay inside the nest, helping with nest duties, feeding the

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:12.220
young, keeping the place clean, building more cells.

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:20.140
After a few days, they begin to venture outside the nest to help in collecting food.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:35.300
If the colony is to be properly nourished, they must gather not only nectar, but pollen.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:41.020
Nectar they transport in their crops, but pollen is held in a tiny ball by a brush of

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.980
stiff hairs on their two hind legs.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:49.540
A worker can carry a lump weighing half as much as she does herself.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:10:08.620
Each bundle is carefully unloaded into one of the storage cells.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:11.340
The pollen isn't eaten by workers.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:16.460
They unselfishly bring it back for the larvae, for its rich in protein and essential food

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:25.420
for their development.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:30.740
By the late summer, there may be more than 200 workers in the nest.

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:36.860
Although the colony is now close to its maximum size, the queen is still laying.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:39.300
But these batches of eggs are different.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:44.420
She's now stopped producing the chemical substance that repressed the sexual development of her

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:50.180
daughters, so these eggs will develop into new queens.

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:56.180
The change affects not just her eggs, but her existing daughters, the workers.

00:10:56.180 --> 00:11:00.700
No longer restrained by the queen's chemical control, some workers have started laying

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.580
their own eggs.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:12.260
This doesn't suit the queen, and she destroys them.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:18.020
The workers haven't made it, but their eggs can develop nonetheless and become males.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:22.860
The queen eats as many of these eggs as she can find because, as well as queen eggs, she's

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:30.600
also producing male eggs and can't tolerate the competition.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.540
She keeps such a close watch that she manages to destroy the workers' eggs almost as soon

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:39.100
as they're laid.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:44.220
The end of summer approaches.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:46.500
There's now anarchy in the colony.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.740
The social order has collapsed.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:55.080
Many of the workers whose eggs are being destroyed by the queen start to attack her.

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:56.980
The onslaught is brutal.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:12:10.780
No quarter is given.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:25.820
Eventually, they sting her to death.

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:30.060
The end of the colony has come.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:36.140
None of the workers will survive the winter, but the young queens will have left the nest

00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:38.260
and found males.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:45.060
It's they who will establish new colonies next spring.

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:47.840
Bumblebees have a particular problem.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:53.540
In any given area, there's only a limited number of holes that are suitable for nests.

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:59.700
European honeybees, which in the wild nest in holes in trees, have similar difficulty.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:06.380
But some bees have adopted a very radical solution, a very brave solution to that difficulty.

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:19.560
They nest out in the open, but at the top of tall trees, sometimes very tall trees.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:24.340
These are the giant Asiatic bees, the biggest of all honeybees.

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:28.940
They're found from the Himalayas all the way down to Southeast Asia.

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:32.620
These colonies are in Malaysia.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:39.120
They defend themselves with stings, very, very powerful stings, which is why I have

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:42.280
to wear a bee suit.

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:47.900
And it's not just against one bee that you have to guard yourself.

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:53.380
Because if one bee attacks you, it releases a pheromone, a chemical signal which is detected

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.760
by the others in the cone.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:01.860
And within seconds, there will be hundreds, indeed probably thousands of them, all around

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:06.260
you launching a mass attack and stinging you.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.940
And some of those stings can actually go through a bee suit.

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:19.420
So something to be avoided.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:48.580
Stinging is a very expensive form of defense because when a bee loses its sting, it dies.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:54.300
So it's better for the colony to warn predators off before they have to fight them off.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:58.580
And they warn them with some dramatic displays.

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:03.580
I've got a reproduction of a hornet, which is one of the main enemies of bees.

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:06.420
I'll see if I can get them to do it.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:07.420
Just watch.

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:08.420
There.

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:13.860
See, there's a moving wave which passes over the surface of the colony.

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:21.020
And that not only produces an impressive pattern, but it also makes it very difficult for any

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:28.220
aggressor like perhaps a hornet, which eats bees, to actually land on that moving carpet

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:33.460
of wings.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:42.080
The colony's great treasure, of course, is its huge store of honey.

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:47.780
This is produced from nectar, which the bees industriously collect from flowers.

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:53.060
They systematically expose it to the air so that the water it contains evaporates and

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:56.620
the nectar becomes sweeter and thicker.

00:15:56.620 --> 00:16:07.460
Eventually it turns into honey.

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:12.160
The combs in which they store it are continuously guarded by the covering of bees.

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:16.700
They cling so thickly that it might seem that nothing could get past them.

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:27.220
But some thieves know how to do so, particularly at night.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:33.300
A death's head hawkmoth flies over the surface of the colony and goes so close to it that

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:39.780
the bees are alarmed enough to wave their warning.

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:44.700
But the moth is not put off.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:48.300
It wants honey.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:58.940
Amazingly, it manages to land on the carpet of bees and quickly pushes its way through

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:00.940
them.

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:07.380
A quick sip of honey and it's off.

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:13.020
It succeeds because although it looks nothing like a bee to our eyes, it has camouflaged

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:19.060
itself with a smell, a pheromone, that convinces the bees that it's one of them.

00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:25.020
But in spite of such raids, bees, thanks to their stings, retain their precious honey.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:33.180
This because it is that that enables them to survive a season without flowers.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:39.940
While some descendants of the wasps became flower foraging bees, others remained hunters

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:43.260
but went down to the ground to search for their prey.

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:48.380
Their wings were more of a hindrance than the help and these insects lost their wings

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:53.740
for most of their lives.

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:59.220
Bees are wood ants and they build nests even bigger than those of the giant bees.

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:06.780
This one is in the pine forests of the Alps.

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:16.340
Hunting parties go out from the nest along well-established trails to search for prey.

00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:23.060
Anything their own size is quickly overpowered.

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:29.260
But by working together, wood ants can tackle prey much bigger than themselves.

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:35.660
Some caterpillars are covered with stinging hairs but the ants cut these off one by one

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:53.500
and they can slice right through a beetle's hard armour.

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:57.580
Now they are attacking another hunter, a spider.

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:01.580
Everything they catch is taken back to the colony to be shared by those workers that

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:16.180
stayed at home looking after the young.

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:21.860
The disadvantage of building a huge nest like this is that you are very obvious to predators

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:40.940
but these ants have a very effective way of defending themselves.

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:47.860
The unmistakable acrid smell of formic acid.

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:53.180
Most ants, like their wasp ancestors, have stings but not these wood ants.

00:19:53.180 --> 00:20:07.100
Instead of injecting poison, they squirt it and very accurately too.

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:09.420
They don't eat just meat.

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:15.360
They also visit aphids that sit in the branches above, drinking the pine tree's sap.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:22.620
This contains more sugar than the aphids need so the ants drink the excess and they collect

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:29.080
it just as fast as the aphids excrete it.

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:33.580
They carry it back to the nest but in this case they transport it inside their swollen

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:34.580
stomachs.

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:44.100
In fact this liquid, honeydew, makes up more than two thirds of the colony's diet.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:49.780
All these wood ant nests are connected to one another by trails and indeed they're

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:52.740
also genetically related to one another.

00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:58.980
There are some 1200 of them in this one patch of forest and that makes this what is thought

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:05.780
to be the biggest super colony of ants in the whole world.

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:10.020
By mid-June the super colony is ready to reproduce.

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:15.420
Out of every nest among the workers come individuals with wings.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:18.760
Some nests produce only males.

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:29.680
They take off in droves.

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:34.540
Other nests produce only females.

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:43.100
Ant sexes, now that they're winged, look remarkably like wasps, a reminder of their

00:21:43.100 --> 00:21:45.620
ancestry.

00:21:45.620 --> 00:22:11.180
Unlike wasps, however, these fliers are not very confident about getting into the air.

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:14.760
Males and females assemble in the nearby meadows.

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:19.860
The queens lay down chemical trails so that the males may quickly discover exactly where

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.260
they are.

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:37.100
And the males are quick to take the hint.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:47.180
The males only live for a few days and they mate as quickly and as frequently as they

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:49.100
can.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:53.860
A queen, on the other hand, may live for as long as 10 years and a single mating will

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:59.380
provide her with enough sperm to last for her entire life.

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:03.420
For a female, mating is often a bit of a battle.

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:06.840
Sometimes she has to bite a male to make him release her.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:16.980
Sometimes she has to hang on to him because he's impatient and wants to move on.

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:20.660
The newly mated queens gather together in the undergrowth.

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:23.220
Here they shed their wings.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:33.340
They found their males, so their travelling is over.

00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:42.240
Now each must find an existing nest in which to lay her eggs.

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:45.080
This one encounters a column of workers.

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:50.680
A wood ant nest may contain as many as a thousand queens, but will these workers allow her to

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:51.780
be one of them?

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:57.480
If they don't, they will bite her to death.

00:23:57.480 --> 00:23:59.040
She's been accepted.

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:03.640
The workers have detected chemical clues on her body that tells them that she's originally

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:06.800
from one of the nests in their super colony.

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:08.880
She's large and fat.

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:10.540
Walking is not easy for her.

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:17.280
A single worker carries her along the trail back home, perhaps even to the same nest in

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:21.740
which she started life.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:25.240
Ants live almost everywhere.

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:32.160
The water falling in this mangrove swamp in Australia exposes in the wet mud an ant's

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:33.720
nest.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:41.800
Every time the tide recedes, the ants must repair any damage the water may have caused.

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:56.840
Collapsed entrances must be reopened and blocked tunnels cleared.

00:24:56.840 --> 00:25:01.480
Now that the mud flats are exposed, the ants hurry to collect what food the tide might

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:05.520
have delivered.

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:16.480
And there are still some stretches of water to be crossed.

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:23.520
The surface tension of the water supports them as they dance across it.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:43.200
Sometimes they actually swim.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:54.080
And there has indeed been a new delivery of food.

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:56.880
But the tide has also created a problem.

00:25:56.880 --> 00:26:01.240
It has washed away the chemical trails that mark the frontiers of their territory, so

00:26:01.240 --> 00:26:08.960
there's now no clear boundary between them and ants belonging to a neighbouring colony.

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:14.360
The interrogation of a stranger is complex and detailed.

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:15.360
Who are you?

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:19.960
Where do you come from?

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:33.040
Answers are readily given and accepted.

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:47.080
But every now and then they have to fight to settle the question.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:51.680
They may have sorted out their disagreement, but now there is a bigger threat to both of

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:52.680
them.

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:54.680
The tide is turning again.

00:26:54.680 --> 00:27:08.800
They must get back to the safety of their nests.

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:13.360
While the tide has been out, larvae and pupae have been moved around the nest to keep them

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:16.280
at the temperature needed for their proper development.

00:27:16.280 --> 00:27:20.800
Now they must be moved again, for the nest is not watertight.

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:23.800
Many of the tunnels and chambers are flooded with every tide.

00:27:23.800 --> 00:27:42.400
There's no time to waste.

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:47.920
But the water doesn't reach every part of the nest, for the ants have constructed bell-shaped

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:54.080
chambers that trap pockets of air and so create refuges where the adults and the young can

00:27:54.080 --> 00:28:12.600
sit out the high tide.

00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:18.240
Here in Arizona, the problem for an ant is not too much water, but too little.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:23.680
The rainfall is so low that there's hardly any vegetation and very little to eat.

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:27.640
So an ant has to be prepared to eat whatever it can find.

00:28:27.640 --> 00:28:34.280
There are seeds, but seeds are very tough and you need very powerful jaws to crack them.

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:44.600
But then that's exactly what these harvest ants have got.

00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:47.640
They make an intensive search of the sand.

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:50.440
Almost any seed will be collected.

00:28:50.440 --> 00:28:52.360
Food around here is very scarce.

00:28:52.360 --> 00:29:06.560
They can't afford to be fussy.

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:11.280
They carry their gleanings back to the nest to store it in ladders, many of which are

00:29:11.280 --> 00:29:17.000
several metres below ground.

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:20.880
But like the mangrove ants, they must work fast.

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:28.240
The desert warms quickly and before long the heat will be intolerable.

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:32.480
By nightfall, the harvesters are back inside the nest.

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:36.440
But there's still a lot going on out in the desert.

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:39.440
There's another ant here too, the night ant.

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:42.320
This is one of their nests in front of me.

00:29:42.320 --> 00:29:45.760
They normally only come out after dark and they're generous.

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:48.140
They'll eat pretty well anything.

00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:51.080
And they have a particular taste for seeds.

00:29:51.080 --> 00:29:56.880
The trouble is that the harvest ants will have gathered all the seeds during the day,

00:29:56.880 --> 00:30:02.640
unless the night ants can do something about it.

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:11.680
Just after dark, the night ants start a major spoiling operation against their rivals.

00:30:11.680 --> 00:30:32.320
They start to shift stones and fragments of plants to block up some holes near their nest.

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:35.440
By morning, it's clear what they've done.

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:47.360
They've trapped the harvesters inside their own nests.

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:52.460
The harvesters now have a lot of work to do before they can get out to collect more seeds.

00:30:52.460 --> 00:31:06.840
They clear away the rubble as quickly as they can.

00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:08.420
But this takes time.

00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:12.400
If they're seriously delayed, the day will be too hot for them to spend time out in the

00:31:12.400 --> 00:31:13.400
open.

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:21.400
So today, they can't collect as much as they normally do.

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:25.760
And that means that by nightfall, there will still be seeds on the ground for the night

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:34.320
ants to collect.

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:37.480
Not all ants live in permanent nests.

00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:44.120
In the tropical forests of Africa and South America, there are some that are nomads.

00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:49.480
These army ants in the rainforests of Central America are camped in the base of a tree.

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:51.640
They've been there for three weeks.

00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:55.920
During this time, the queen has been laying eggs, several thousand a day.

00:31:55.920 --> 00:32:00.120
The army has also been ransacking the surrounding forest for prey.

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:03.680
But now it's time for them to find new hunting grounds.

00:32:03.680 --> 00:32:18.680
So once more, they start to march.

00:32:18.680 --> 00:32:24.560
The site for the new Bivouac has not been picked by the queen, but by the workers.

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:29.720
Scouts have been exploring the neighborhood, and they've decided on a new place.

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:35.440
And now their chemical trails are leading the whole colony from the old Bivouac to the

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:39.320
new one.

00:32:39.320 --> 00:32:44.160
As in an army, the soldiers are prepared to risk their lives for the common good.

00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:48.480
A group of them interlock their bodies to form a safety barrier that will catch any

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:53.280
of their companions that might slip off this sloping trunk.

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:58.840
They take everything with them, larvae, food, and in this case, as very rarely seen, winged

00:32:58.840 --> 00:33:04.120
males.

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:19.280
By the time daylight comes, the army has established a new Bivouac.

00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:33.400
Its walls and tunnels are formed by the interlinked bodies of hundreds and thousands of individuals.

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:35.760
And this is only a temporary camp.

00:33:35.760 --> 00:33:38.920
They still haven't reached fresh hunting grounds.

00:33:38.920 --> 00:34:04.120
Even so, they must eat, and the workers set off to find food.

00:34:04.120 --> 00:34:17.880
There are probably a million individual ants in this one colony, and together they're collaborating

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:22.800
and cooperating so that the colony has become one great superorganism.

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:25.960
There's no central controlling intelligence as such.

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:31.600
Instead, the behavior of the superorganism is the cumulative result of thousands upon

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:35.320
thousands of tiny, mini decisions by individual ants.

00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:40.240
The worker moves forward into new territory, leading a chemical trail behind it.

00:34:40.240 --> 00:34:44.400
And then another following in its trail advances still a little further.

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:56.640
So the superorganism as a whole is moving through the forest, searching for food.

00:34:56.640 --> 00:35:01.280
These hunters can subdue almost any other creature in the undergrowth.

00:35:01.280 --> 00:35:05.840
Young predators may be armed with virulent poisons, but their attackers are too small

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:07.840
to sting.

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:25.000
A lizard has no defense at all.

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:37.960
A special cast of workers with particularly large jaws protect the smaller workers as

00:35:37.960 --> 00:35:48.840
they sting their prey and butcher it.

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:57.600
The venom in their stings liquefies the tissues of their victims so that the bodies are more

00:35:57.600 --> 00:36:19.840
easily cut up into smaller pieces for transport.

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:26.080
The chemical trails laid down by the first scouts have now been strengthened and broadened

00:36:26.080 --> 00:36:28.720
by the passage of many, many more workers.

00:36:28.720 --> 00:36:33.920
And now those trails are serving as highways along which Booty is being brought back to

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:56.760
give their dweck to feed the young brood.

00:36:56.760 --> 00:37:02.280
Remarkably, almost as soon as these workers return with food, scouts begin to search for

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:05.600
a new bivouac site.

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:10.800
The colony will move again tonight and every night for the next few weeks until the queen

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:16.200
is ready to lay more eggs.

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:23.200
When it comes to creating a permanent home for a colony, the champions by far are these

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:27.120
tiny creatures, termites.

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:32.520
Unlike ants, all termites are vegetarians, they are in fact descended not from wasps

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:38.120
but from cockroaches and their huge nests act not only as their fortresses but their

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:40.960
food stores.

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:46.440
They build with nothing but mud and their own excrement, yet their nests are gigantic.

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:51.560
If termites were our size, some of their homes would be four times as tall as New York's

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:56.160
skyscrapers and measure up to five miles across at their base.

00:37:56.160 --> 00:38:03.800
These are not quite so tall but they are particularly remarkable for another reason.

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:09.920
Every one of these termite hills points in the same direction, north and south.

00:38:09.920 --> 00:38:17.320
It's as though they were needles in a compass and indeed they are called magnetic termites.

00:38:17.320 --> 00:38:24.440
They in fact take their cue for building from the magnetism of the earth but the benefit

00:38:24.440 --> 00:38:41.920
of doing so comes not from that but from the daily movement of the sun.

00:38:41.920 --> 00:38:47.720
In the morning, the rays of the rising sun strike the eastern faces of the mound four

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:53.520
square and the termites, after the cold of the night, need warming up and are gathered

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:56.760
in galleries immediately below the surface.

00:38:56.760 --> 00:39:01.760
But as the day continues, it warms up but the termites don't overheat because the rays

00:39:01.760 --> 00:39:08.040
of the sun only strike the surface dancingly and by midday, the full force of the sun is

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:20.280
felt only on the top edge.

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:27.560
As the sun moves towards the west, so this face becomes roastingly hot but the eastern

00:39:27.560 --> 00:39:34.680
face falls into shadow and remains relatively cool and the termites stay at the temperature

00:39:34.680 --> 00:39:37.760
that suits them best.

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:42.280
Other termites escape the heat of the day by retreating to deep cellars below their

00:39:42.280 --> 00:39:48.760
mounds but these magnetic termites colonise areas that flood during the rainy season and

00:39:48.760 --> 00:39:52.280
the ground beneath them is regularly waterlogged.

00:39:52.280 --> 00:39:58.200
So their compass-like mounds are a response not just to the movement of the sun but to

00:39:58.200 --> 00:40:01.360
badly drained sites.

00:40:01.360 --> 00:40:07.760
Here in South Africa, it can also get very hot but there's no danger of flooding so termites

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:14.800
can take refuge from the heat below ground where it's cool and relatively stable.

00:40:14.800 --> 00:40:20.200
But two million insects living below ground create a different kind of problem.

00:40:20.200 --> 00:40:23.080
The air around them gets stale.

00:40:23.080 --> 00:40:29.240
So termites need to have a way of linking the underground air with the fresh air above,

00:40:29.240 --> 00:40:34.680
a ventilation system and they do that with this.

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:42.080
And to see how it works, you've got to look inside.

00:40:42.080 --> 00:40:48.000
Using the latest scanning techniques, we can create a picture of the mound's interior.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:54.320
An intricate network of passages lead to a central chimney.

00:40:54.320 --> 00:41:03.800
Hot stale air from the insect population below rises up through the chimney.

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:07.240
But the top of the mound is sealed.

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:10.880
So how does this stale air escape?

00:41:10.880 --> 00:41:15.560
The mound may look as though it has strong defensive walls like a fortress but in fact

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:22.120
these walls are porous and their primary purpose is to harness the wind.

00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:26.280
Fresh air blowing against the side of the mound is forced through the tiny holes in

00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:27.520
these walls.

00:41:27.520 --> 00:41:34.520
From there, it travels through the smaller tunnels until it reaches the central chimney.

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:40.720
Here the cooler fresh air mixes with the hot stale air rising from the insect community

00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:42.040
below.

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:46.960
Meanwhile, some air is blown around the side of the mound.

00:41:46.960 --> 00:41:52.280
This creates a suction that pulls the stale air out of the chimney and out through the

00:41:52.280 --> 00:41:53.760
outer walls.

00:41:53.760 --> 00:42:00.320
So an internal air current is created and the whole mound ventilated.

00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:07.080
The mound's inhabitants spend most of their time close to or below ground level.

00:42:07.080 --> 00:42:12.800
Beneath their living quarters there are garden chambers where the termites cultivate a fungus

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:18.720
that rots the wood and vegetation they collect and make it digestible.

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:23.920
Farther down still, the queen lies in her own chamber.

00:42:23.920 --> 00:42:29.480
Her huge body is a gigantic egg producing factory.

00:42:29.480 --> 00:42:33.240
She's so swollen that she can't look after herself.

00:42:33.240 --> 00:42:39.760
The workers must constantly clean her and feed her with food from their own crops.

00:42:39.760 --> 00:42:45.240
Her partner, with whom she founded the colony maybe 20 years ago, is still with her and

00:42:45.240 --> 00:42:50.160
mates with her throughout her life.

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:57.720
She lays eggs at an extraordinary rate, as many as 30,000 a day.

00:42:57.720 --> 00:43:03.320
As she produces them, so workers remove them from the royal chamber and take them to nurseries.

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:15.000
There they'll be fed on compost from the fungus gardens until they turn into adults.

00:43:15.000 --> 00:43:21.240
The superorganism that lives in this great castle crops the surrounding vegetation just

00:43:21.240 --> 00:43:24.880
about as severely as an antelope.

00:43:24.880 --> 00:43:31.240
The density of individual termites around here is extraordinary, over 100,000 per square

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:32.840
metre.

00:43:32.840 --> 00:43:39.560
And just as there are lions and leopard that hunt antelope, so in the undergrowth there

00:43:39.560 --> 00:43:46.760
are insect hunters which prey on the tiny herbivores, the ants, the termites' ancient

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:49.760
enemy.

00:43:49.760 --> 00:43:55.160
The Belyants, specialist termite hunters.

00:43:55.160 --> 00:44:06.960
A scout has laid down a clear chemical trail and this battalion of workers have picked

00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:09.320
it up and are following it.

00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:14.360
There may be only a few hundred of them, but they're going to severely test the defences

00:44:14.360 --> 00:44:23.160
of a termite colony.

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:39.600
The mound has formidable guards, soldier termites.

00:44:39.600 --> 00:44:42.760
The ants have a special technique for dealing with these soldiers.

00:44:42.760 --> 00:44:48.360
They grab the termite's jaw and then sting it in the only vulnerable place on its head,

00:44:48.360 --> 00:44:57.760
in its mouth.

00:44:57.760 --> 00:45:00.680
The ants' front line breaks into the colony.

00:45:00.680 --> 00:45:04.400
Reinforcements for the termite soldiers arrive quickly.

00:45:04.400 --> 00:45:10.760
Already there are casualties on both sides.

00:45:10.760 --> 00:45:19.600
But the invaders overwhelm the defenders.

00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:24.520
It's not to the ants' advantage to kill an entire termite colony any more than it would

00:45:24.520 --> 00:45:27.800
be sensible for farmers to exterminate their cattle.

00:45:27.800 --> 00:45:32.160
Better to let most survive so that they can be regularly raided.

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:36.680
So although there are millions of termites in the colony, the Matabele ants rarely go

00:45:36.680 --> 00:45:58.200
deep into the nest to press home their victory.

00:45:58.200 --> 00:46:01.360
The raid lasts less than 15 minutes.

00:46:01.360 --> 00:46:14.560
Nonetheless, the spoils are impressive.

00:46:14.560 --> 00:46:28.280
Termite bodies are now being piled in dumps outside the nest.

00:46:28.280 --> 00:46:33.400
Many of the casualties are still alive but paralysed by the ants' stings.

00:46:33.400 --> 00:46:42.360
Now the raiders have the considerable task of carrying their victims back to their nest.

00:46:42.360 --> 00:46:44.880
They will have to take all their booty with them.

00:46:44.880 --> 00:46:53.560
If any termite bodies are left behind, they will be collected by scavengers.

00:46:53.560 --> 00:46:56.600
The termite soldiers certainly fought hard.

00:46:56.600 --> 00:47:02.320
One of their dead still grips a Matabele soldier in its jaws, which it killed before it was

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:05.320
itself slaughtered.

00:47:05.320 --> 00:47:15.440
Well, it's been a successful raid.

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:18.960
Many of the bigger ones have got mouthfuls of termites.

00:47:18.960 --> 00:47:23.640
How they managed to hold all of them in one mouthful, I don't know.

00:47:23.640 --> 00:47:29.960
Obviously, they've got a little way to go now and soon the young ones back in the nest

00:47:29.960 --> 00:47:34.320
will be getting good food.

00:47:34.320 --> 00:47:38.320
The Matabele ants will use their plunder to raise more workers.

00:47:38.320 --> 00:47:42.200
Ironically, the raid will have the same effect on the termites.

00:47:42.200 --> 00:47:46.880
The queen will detect the loss of her soldiers and workers and will increase her output of

00:47:46.880 --> 00:47:49.640
eggs to repopulate the colony.

00:47:49.640 --> 00:47:59.720
So there will be just as much food for the Matabele the next time they raid.

00:47:59.720 --> 00:48:05.640
The tiny creatures of the undergrowth were the first animals of any kind to colonise

00:48:05.640 --> 00:48:07.280
the land.

00:48:07.280 --> 00:48:12.120
They established the foundations of the land's ecosystems.

00:48:12.120 --> 00:48:18.440
Ultimately, they were able to transcend any limitations of their small size by banding

00:48:18.440 --> 00:48:24.280
together in huge communities of millions and putting up buildings like this one.

00:48:24.280 --> 00:48:29.880
If we and the rest of the backbone animals were to disappear overnight, the rest of the

00:48:29.880 --> 00:48:33.040
world would get on pretty well.

00:48:33.040 --> 00:48:39.040
But if they were to disappear, the land's ecosystems would collapse.

00:48:39.040 --> 00:48:41.680
The soil would lose its fertility.

00:48:41.680 --> 00:48:45.040
Many of the plants would no longer be pollinated.

00:48:45.040 --> 00:48:50.600
Plants of animals, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals, would have nothing to eat.

00:48:50.600 --> 00:48:56.720
And our fields and pastures would be covered with dung and carrion.

00:48:56.720 --> 00:49:03.880
These small creatures are within a few inches of our feet wherever we go on land, but often

00:49:03.880 --> 00:49:06.280
they're disregarded.

00:49:06.280 --> 00:49:28.880
We would do very well to remember them.

00:49:28.880 --> 00:49:33.040
Throughout life in the undergrowth, we've seen the most extraordinary creatures living

00:49:33.040 --> 00:49:40.440
their most remarkable lives right under our notice.

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:45.880
But what's most remarkable is that they're just the tip of the iceberg.

00:49:45.880 --> 00:49:50.400
Of all the animals on the planet, land-living invertebrates are the most numerous, both

00:49:50.400 --> 00:49:52.960
in kinds and absolute numbers.

00:49:52.960 --> 00:49:57.760
But incredibly, we still know the least about them.

00:49:57.760 --> 00:50:04.280
Thankfully, as we found out in Fly on the Wall, behind the scenes there are people out

00:50:04.280 --> 00:50:12.080
there every day pushing the boundaries of our knowledge.

00:50:12.080 --> 00:50:19.560
You might be surprised to hear that even the world's largest insect is still almost unknown.

00:50:19.560 --> 00:50:28.200
But in the depths of the Amazon, one man is trying to piece together a picture of its

00:50:28.200 --> 00:50:29.200
life.

00:50:29.200 --> 00:50:34.240
Scientist Frank Hervor is on the trail of Titanus, the Titan beetle.

00:50:34.240 --> 00:50:39.400
Titanus isn't spectacular because of its beautiful colors or ornate structures.

00:50:39.400 --> 00:50:42.520
It's spectacular because it's the size of your shoe.

00:50:42.520 --> 00:50:47.880
There's a magic to that, to those people who still have the little child inside them alive

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:55.640
at all times and are fascinated, more over-thrilled at the prospect of seeing something like this

00:50:55.640 --> 00:50:57.760
in the wild.

00:50:57.760 --> 00:51:02.680
But how do you find one particular beetle in the Amazon?

00:51:02.680 --> 00:51:05.600
Frank's answer is light.

00:51:05.600 --> 00:51:10.040
A bright light and a white sheet and a forest clearing is all you need.

00:51:10.040 --> 00:51:14.640
The hope that if you wait long enough, the Titan will be lured into the light and will

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:16.940
find you.

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:22.240
And it's not only Titans that are attracted to light.

00:51:22.240 --> 00:51:27.000
Frank is a world authority on the insects of the Amazon and so for him, this is all

00:51:27.000 --> 00:51:31.800
part of the thrill of a Titan hunt.

00:51:31.800 --> 00:51:35.600
Every night there is something different.

00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:40.680
Among these creatures, there may well be a dozen or so that no scientist has ever seen

00:51:40.680 --> 00:51:43.680
before, let alone understood.

00:51:43.680 --> 00:51:49.480
Ten inches, something.

00:51:49.480 --> 00:51:53.120
That's an amazing nectar gathering too.

00:51:53.120 --> 00:51:56.920
You would guess there must be a flower out here, probably a tree, maybe big noniaceae

00:51:56.920 --> 00:52:01.320
or something that has an enormous corolla to the flower.

00:52:01.320 --> 00:52:04.720
Now watch, it'll just coil it right back up.

00:52:04.720 --> 00:52:11.720
Unharmed, unperturbed.

00:52:11.720 --> 00:52:15.240
It's nearly dawn and still no Titan.

00:52:15.240 --> 00:52:25.480
But beetles love moisture, so the rain is a good omen for tomorrow night.

00:52:25.480 --> 00:52:29.440
The Titan lives only for two weeks as a winged adult.

00:52:29.440 --> 00:52:34.560
Like other beetles, it must spend most of its life, possibly years, as a larva, but

00:52:34.560 --> 00:52:39.280
no one has ever seen it.

00:52:39.280 --> 00:52:45.480
By looking for other beetle larvae, Frank can get an idea of how big it must be.

00:52:45.480 --> 00:52:49.400
Oh, here we go.

00:52:49.400 --> 00:52:53.280
Here's our larva right here.

